   "text": "This is my reading of the drawing: a lipid A\u2013type diglucosamine bisphosphate, written as its ammonium salt. It is a monophosphoryl lipid A with six acyl chains. I could not count the chain carbons exactly from the image, so the chain lengths are an assumption.\n\n**Sugar core**\n- The reducing-end glucosamine (left ring) has a free anomeric OH. Its C2 carries an amide, C3 carries an ester, C4 is OH, and C6 forms the glycosidic link to the other sugar.\n- The second glucosamine (right ring) is \u03b2-linked through its C1 oxygen to C6 of the left ring. Its C2 carries an amide, C3 carries an ester, C4 carries the phosphate (one OH and one O\u207b), and C6 is a free CH2OH.\n\n**Acyl chains**\n- The left ring has two 3-hydroxytetradecanoyl groups, one as an amide on N2 and one as an ester on O3.\n- The right ring has two 3-(tetradecanoyloxy)tetradecanoyl groups, one as an amide on N2 and one as an ester on O3.\n- I assumed C14 for every chain, including the two secondary myristoyl groups. The counted lengths in the drawing were ambiguous.\n\n**Stereochemistry**\n- I assigned the sugar centres by analogy with \u03b2-D-glucosamine rather than reading each wedge individually.\n- I assigned R at every 3-hydroxy and 3-acyloxy carbon, which is the usual lipid A configuration.\n\nSMILES: OC[C@H]1O[C@@H](OC[C@H]2O[C@@H](O)[C@H](NC(=O)C[C@H](O)CCCCCCCCCCC)[C@@H](OC(=O)C[C@H](O)CCCCCCCCCCC)[C@@H]2O)[C@H](NC(=O)C[C@H](OC(=O)CCCCCCCCCCCCC)CCCCCCCCCCC)[C@@H](OC(=O)C[C@H](OC(=O)CCCCCCCCCCCCC)CCCCCCCCCCC)[C@@H]1OP(=O)(O)[O-].[NH4+]"
